NNAAMMEE
     rrddaattee — set the system's date from a remote host

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     rrddaattee [--4466aaccnnooppssvv] _h_o_s_t

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     rrddaattee displays and sets the local date and time from the host name or ad‐
     dress given as the argument.  The time source may be an RFC 5905 protocol
     SNTP/NTP server or an RFC 868 TCP protocol server, which is usually im‐
     plemented as a built-in service of inetd(8).  By default, rrddaattee uses the
     RFC 5905 SNTP/NTP protocol.

     The options are as follows:

     --44      Forces rrddaattee to use IPv4 addresses only.

     --66      Forces rrddaattee to use IPv6 addresses only.

     --aa      Use the adjtime(2) call to gradually skew the local time to the
             remote time rather than just hopping.

     --cc      Correct leap seconds.  This should be used only when synchroniz‐
             ing to a server which does not correctly account for leap sec‐
             onds.

     --nn      Use SNTP (RFC 5905) instead of the RFC 868 time protocol.  This
             is the default.  This protocol counts 32 bits of seconds from
             January 1, 1900 and will handle rollover to a new NTP era in Feb‐
             ruary 2036.

     --oo      Use an RFC 868 TCP protocol server instead of SNTP.  This proto‐
             col is obsolete as it is not capable of representing dates past
             January 19, 2038 03:14:07 GMT.

     --pp      Do not set, just print the remote time.

     --ss      Do not print the time.

     --vv      Verbose output.  Always show the adjustment.

FFIILLEESS
     /var/log/wtmp  record of date resets and time changes

